  "text": "The study measured how much radioactive strontium was held by two different clay materials when they were pre‑loaded with various amounts of hydrogen, calcium, barium or potassium ions. It also tracked how much of the metal the test plants absorbed over an eight‑hour period from both the clay suspensions and from simple solutions that contained the same total ion concentrations. In a separate set of experiments, a soil that had been treated to contain different levels of readily exchangeable calcium was mixed with a strontium salt solution to see how much metal would stick to the soil particles. The layered clay type retained less strontium and allowed the plants to take up more of it than the swelling clay type. Adding calcium or barium, rather than hydrogen, lowered the amount of strontium that stayed on the clays while raising the amount taken up by the plants, thereby speeding the clean‑up process. The swelling clay bound strontium with enough strength to limit plant uptake, a behavior not seen with the layered clay. Changing the amount of exchangeable calcium in the soil did not noticeably alter how much strontium was adsorbed from the solution."
